<%# Every marketing page, rendered from its row.

  ONE WRAPPER FOR ALL 48 PAGES. The standing pages and the catalogue detail pages differ in exactly one way:
  a detail page has a catalogue entry beside it, which contributes the availability badge, the prerequisite
  callout, the documentation link and the neighbours. Everything else -- heading, standfirst, sections -- is
  the row. Two templates would be two places to fix the next time the chrome changes.

  WHAT COMES FROM THE OPERATOR AND WHAT DOES NOT, visible in one screen: `page.heading`, `page.lede` and
  `page.sections` are the row; `entry.state`, the `requires` line, the docs slug and the related ids are
  Marketing::Catalog. An operator cannot edit a feature's availability, because it was never theirs.

  THE SECTIONS ARE NOT INSIDE A MEASURED COLUMN ANY MORE, and that is the change this page's rhythm hangs
  on. Every block used to sit in one `max-w-6xl` article on one colour, so a nine-section page was a single
  column of hairline-separated text with no way to tell a subject from the next. Each section is now a
  full-bleed band that carries its own field and its own measure (shared/_sections.html.erb), and what is
  left here is the page chrome: the breadcrumb, the heading, the prerequisite, and the catalogue's own
  footer blocks. They keep the same `max-w-6xl` and the same gutters as a band's interior, so every left
  edge on the page still lines up.
%>
<% entry = local_assigns[:entry] %>
<% scope = local_assigns[:scope] %>
<% measure = 'mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6' %>

<%# A PAGE THAT OPENS WITH A HERO LETS THE HERO CARRY ITS HEADING.

    The heading and the standfirst are still the row's own columns and there is still exactly one H1 -- the
    only question is which template prints it. When the first section is a `hero`, printing it here would put
    the eyebrow below the standfirst and leave the actions floating away from the sentence they belong to,
    which is what the old homepage did. So a leading hero is rendered first, full-bleed, and it prints the
    heading itself; every other page keeps the header below and renders every section inside the article. %>
<% sections = Array(page.sections) %>
<% lead_hero = sections.first if sections.first&.fetch('type', nil) == 'hero' %>
<% body_sections = lead_hero ? sections.drop(1) : sections %>

<article>
  <% if lead_hero %>
    <%= render 'marketing/sections/hero', section: lead_hero, page: page %>
  <% end %>

  <% if entry && scope %>
    <div class="<%= measure %> pt-8">
      <% trail = marketing_breadcrumb(scope, t("marketing.#{scope}.#{entry.id}.name")) %>
      <%= render 'marketing/shared/breadcrumb', trail: trail %>
      <%# The machine-readable half of the SAME trail, built from the same array, so a search result's
          breadcrumb shows what the page shows. `content_for` because it belongs in the head, and the
          template renders before the layout. %>
      <% content_for :structured_data do %>
        <%= marketing_structured_data(marketing_breadcrumb_schema(trail, marketing_url(marketing_page))) %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <% unless lead_hero %>
    <header class="<%= measure %> <%= entry && scope ? 'pt-6' : 'pt-16' %>">
      <%# THE CHANNEL'S OWN MARK ABOVE ITS NAME. A reader arriving on /channels/whatsapp has usually come
          looking for one specific app, and the mark confirms they are in the right place before a word of
          the page is read. Only channels carry one: a feature or a solution is not a brand.

          `entry &&` is the whole reason this page still renders. The catalogue entry is OPTIONAL on a
          detail page -- Marketing::EntriesController#show says so, and Marketing::Catalog.find answers nil
          for any slug the catalogue does not hold -- so an operator renaming a channel page's slug, which
          SitePage::RENAMEABLE_KINDS explicitly allows, left `entry.id` raising on a public URL. Measured:
          a kind:'channel' row at a fresh slug answered 500 with "undefined method 'id' for nil". Every
          other `entry` reference in this file was already guarded; this one was not. %>
      <% if scope == :channels && entry && Marketing::ChannelBrand.marked?(entry.id) %>
        <div class="mb-6">
          <%= render 'marketing/shared/channel_mark', id: entry.id, size: 56, labelled: false %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
      <h1 class="text-balance text-[2.5rem] font-bold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-lyn-ink sm:text-[3.25rem]">
        <%= page.heading.presence || page.title %>
      </h1>

      <%# THE AVAILABILITY BADGE SITS BESIDE THE TITLE, NOT INSIDE IT. It used to be a flex child of the H1,
          which was survivable while the heading was 30px and is not now that it is 52px: `items-center`
          hung a 12px chip in the middle of a display headline. It is also not part of the page's NAME,
          which is what an H1 states -- it is a fact about the thing the page is about.

          `capture` rather than a second copy of "which states render": an available entry renders nothing
          at all, and whitespace is blank, so the row is drawn only when the badge has something to say.
          The partial stays the one place that decides what that is. %>
      <% badge = entry ? capture { render 'marketing/shared/state_badge', state: entry.state } : nil %>
      <% if badge.present? %>
        <p class="mt-5"><%= badge %></p>
      <% end %>
      <% if page.lede.present? %>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-[1.8] text-lyn-ink-soft"><%= page.lede %></p>
      <% end %>
    </header>
  <% end %>

  <%# The prerequisite, which is what makes the sentences above it true. From the catalogue, never the row.

      MARKED LIKE AN ASIDE IN A DOCUMENT, NOT A TINTED SLAB IN AN ADMIN SCREEN. This was a tinted box in
      a colour from outside the ramp -- the one off-system colour field on the page, and exactly the
      treatment sections/_note.html.erb rejects for the same reason. It now takes that partial's rule on the
      start edge and the catalogue card's inline label, so the sentence that decides whether a reader can
      use this feature today does not get two incompatible looks depending on which page they met it on. %>
  <% if entry && t("marketing.#{scope}.#{entry.id}.requires", default: nil).present? %>
    <div class="<%= measure %> mt-10">
      <p class="max-w-2xl border-s-2 border-lyn-royal/30 ps-5 text-base leading-[1.8] text-lyn-ink-soft">
        <span class="font-semibold text-lyn-ink">
          <%= t(scope == :channels ? 'marketing.state.you_supply' : 'marketing.state.requires') %>
        </span>
        <%= t("marketing.#{scope}.#{entry.id}.requires") %>
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render 'marketing/shared/sections', sections: body_sections, page: page %>

  <%# THE DOCUMENTATION LINK RESOLVES TO THE READER'S OWN LANGUAGE. `entry.docs` names the article's
      locale-independent key, not its slug -- see MarketingHelper#marketing_docs_article -- so it is resolved
      here and the block is drawn only when a published article answers it. %>
  <% docs_article = entry&.docs && marketing_docs_article(entry.docs) %>

  <% if docs_article || entry&.channel_ids&.any? || entry&.related_ids&.any? %>
    <div class="<%= measure %> py-16">
      <% if docs_article %>
        <p>
          <a class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 rounded-xl border border-lyn-line px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-lyn-ink transition-colors hover:border-lyn-royal hover:text-lyn-royal focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-lyn-blue"
             href="<%= lynomia_docs_article_path(docs_article.slug) %>">
            <%= t('marketing.read_docs') %>
          </a>
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <% if entry&.channel_ids&.any? %>
        <section class="mt-12">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-lyn-ink"><%= t('marketing.detail.works_with') %></h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            <% entry.channel_ids.each do |channel_id| %>
              <% channel = Marketing::Catalog.channel(channel_id) %>
              <li>
                <a class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-lyn-line py-1 pe-4 ps-1 text-sm font-medium text-lyn-ink-soft transition-colors hover:border-lyn-royal hover:text-lyn-royal focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-lyn-blue"
                   href="<%= marketing_entry_path(:channels, channel) %>">
                  <% if Marketing::ChannelBrand.marked?(channel.id) %>
                    <%= render 'marketing/shared/channel_mark', id: channel.id, size: 28, labelled: false %>
                  <% end %>
                  <%= t("marketing.channels.#{channel.id}.name") %>
                </a>
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
        </section>
      <% end %>

      <% if entry&.related_ids&.any? %>
        <section class="mt-12">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-lyn-ink"><%= t('marketing.detail.read_next') %></h2>
          <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
            <% entry.related_ids.each do |feature_id| %>
              <% feature = Marketing::Catalog.feature(feature_id) %>
              <a class="group rounded-2xl border border-lyn-line p-5 transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-lyn-royal/40 hover:shadow-[0_18px_40px_-26px_rgba(10,23,51,0.45)] focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-lyn-blue"
                 href="<%= marketing_entry_path(:features, feature) %>">
                <span class="block text-base font-semibold text-lyn-ink"><%= t("marketing.features.#{feature.id}.name") %></span>
                <span class="mt-1.5 block text-sm leading-[1.8] text-lyn-ink-soft"><%= t("marketing.features.#{feature.id}.summary") %></span>
              </a>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </section>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</article>

<%= render 'marketing/shared/cta' %>
